   "newText": "This confirms the shared-color intersection rule for a vertical divider too; the mask can be sparse or empty. Exclude the divider and align panels before evaluating the shared-color cells.\n\n## Stamp a short line motif onto repeated marker pixels (task `task-797e18b4`, verified correct)\n\nA sparse, uniform-background grid contains a short line motif and scattered marker pixels. Replicate the motif at each marker location, using the motif orientation and colors, replacing the markers; preserve background and unrelated cells. The test input was 15x28, background 1, with scattered color-5 markers and a three-cell horizontal color-8 motif at row 2, columns 17–19. The accepted output replaced every 5 with a horizontal length-3 run of 8s centered on that marker, preserving all other 1s and the source motif. For example, a marker at row 0, column 5 became 8s at columns 4–6; this was done for every color-5 marker, including markers near edges where the run remained in bounds. Output dimensions were unchanged.\n\nDemo 2 supports that the motif can have different colors rather than being a single-color bar: background 6, a vertical motif `[5,2,7]` at rows 2–4 in column 24, and scattered color-4 marker cells. Output stamps that vertical sequence upward from each marker so the marker coordinate is replaced by the motif's bottom value 7, the cell immediately above is 2, and the next above is 5 (e.g. marker at row 4, column 6 produced 5,2,7 in rows 2–4 of column 6). The motif already present at column 24 remained. This confirms orientation and sequence/color replication, but gives a different anchor convention than the symmetric monochrome horizontal test: use demonstrated motif geometry/anchor carefully and avoid assuming all seed types align the same way.\n\nDemo 1 (18x13, background 0) had a compact multicolor pattern around rows 3–7, columns 3–7, and its output was identical to its input; do not infer that every task instance necessarily modifies every pattern. The task test and demo 2 are strong evidence for stamping a short motif at repeated distinct marker pixels, while demo 1 indicates a no-op case or an instance where no stamp trigger applied. The task test was solved with zero wrong submissions; the exact accepted output consisted of 8 triplets at the five original marker positions plus the original source triplet."
